WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.128
Content Security Policy helps browsers
enforce resource-loading rules and reduce the

2
00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:10.992
impact of some injection flaws.

3
00:00:11.072 --> 00:00:17.257
It can restrict script sources, style
sources, image sources, frames,

4
00:00:17.337 --> 00:00:23.419
fonts, connections, form destinations, and
other browser behavior.

5
00:00:23.499 --> 00:00:27.210
CSP is not a replacement for secure
coding.

6
00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:33.269
It is most useful when the application
already uses output encoding,

7
00:00:33.349 --> 00:00:40.359
safe rendering patterns, secure framework
practices, dependency management,

8
00:00:40.439 --> 00:00:43.016
and careful frontend design.

9
00:00:43.096 --> 00:00:47.838
Strong CSP design usually starts in
report-only mode,

10
00:00:47.918 --> 00:00:55.134
then moves toward enforcement as the team
understands legitimate resource needs.

11
00:00:55.214 --> 00:01:01.605
Risky allowances such as unsafe-inline,
unsafe-eval, broad wildcards,

12
00:01:01.685 --> 00:01:07.458
and unnecessary third-party sources should
be reduced over time.

13
00:01:07.538 --> 00:01:13.826
Nonces and hashes can support trusted
inline content when appropriate.

14
00:01:13.906 --> 00:01:21.226
Reporting and monitoring help teams
improve policies, understand frontend drift,

15
00:01:21.306 --> 00:01:24.604
and catch unexpected resource loads.

16
00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:30.045
The best CSP programs are maintained,
owned, and documented.

17
00:01:30.125 --> 00:01:37.135
They treat the policy as part of
application architecture rather than a one-time

18
00:01:37.215 --> 00:01:37.936
header.

19
00:01:38.016 --> 00:01:44.098
Developers review templates and script
usage, test important pages,

20
00:01:44.178 --> 00:01:50.157
track third-party origins, and update
policies as features change.

21
00:01:50.237 --> 00:01:57.660
The goal is a browser-enforced boundary
that supports the application?s real design

22
00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:03.512
while making unexpected code and content
harder to run unnoticed.

23
00:02:03.592 --> 00:02:10.808
Operationally, CSP works best when changes
are treated like application changes.

24
00:02:10.888 --> 00:02:17.074
New scripts, widgets, analytics tools,
frame targets, API endpoints,

25
00:02:17.154 --> 00:02:23.545
and style patterns should be reviewed
before they are added to the policy.

26
00:02:23.625 --> 00:02:30.532
That review keeps the header from becoming
a broad compatibility list and helps

27
00:02:30.612 --> 00:02:36.900
teams preserve the security value they
worked to create during rollout.
